<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gudea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2100 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/22nd-century-bce">22nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gudea was a ruler of the Sumerian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> around 2100 BCE, in the period between the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> and the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>. He is known for an unusual number of temple-building inscriptions and some two dozen diorite statues showing him in prayer, most of them now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea-cylinders">Gudea Cylinders</a> describe the rebuilding of the Eninnu temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gudea.jpg" alt="Gudea" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jastrow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gudea_of_Lagash_Girsu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gudea</b> campaigned to the east against Anshan and Elam; his sixth year was named for the smiting of Anshan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gudea</b>, ruler of Lagash around 2100 BCE, left long Sumerian inscriptions on statues and clay cylinders, and the Third Dynasty of Ur, which ruled Mesopotamia from about 2112 to 2004 BCE, used Sumerian for a vast administration that has left tens of thousands of tablets, as well as…</blockquote><a href="/language/sumerian">Sumerian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Akkadian Empire, claimed that ships from Meluhha, Magan and Dilmun moored at the quay of his capital, Akkad, and <b>Gudea</b>, ruler of Lagash in the late 3rd millennium BCE, wrote of lapis lazuli and bright carnelian from Meluhha; other texts mention Meluhhan gold dust and fine woods.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Some kings, such as Erridupizir, left Akkadian inscriptions at Nippur imitating Akkadian royal style, but southern cities like Lagash under <b>Gudea</b> and Ur and Uruk carried on largely independently.</blockquote><a href="/state/gutian-rule">Gutian rule</a></li></ul></section>
